## Citation, requested and not required

CC0 asks nothing of you. This does:

> Picture Wrap, "The Sawdust Trail" (Q18153746), wrapped 5 October 1974.
> Consulted 1 August 2026. Derived from Wikidata and TMDB.

Cite the picture's Wikidata identifier and the date you consulted it. The
underlying databases change, and a claim here is only as current as the
day it was read.

## What you should know before relying on it

**A death date can be wrong, and roughly one closing in thirteen rests on
a date only TMDB records.** Where Wikidata and TMDB disagree, the corpus
publishes the disagreement rather than picking a winner; 1,034 closings
carry a `disputed` mark and `pass/provenance-disputes.tsv` names the 337
people behind them. In at least a few dozen cases the archive is known to
carry a typo it inherited.

**Cast lists are incomplete.** Below-the-line crew does not exist in any
free database. A picture can read as closed while people who were never
entered anywhere are alive. This is the project's central limitation and
no licence changes it.

**A closing needs a recorded death.** Pictures where nobody is recorded
dead and nobody is recorded living are published as *unclassified* rather
than wrapped, because an absence of evidence is not evidence.

`docs/METHOD.md` is the full account of how a wrap is decided.

## Why CC0 despite TMDB's terms

TMDB's API terms forbid commercial use, derivatives and AI training, and
CC0 grants all three. That looks like a conflict and mostly is not.

A death date is a fact. TMDB holds no copyright in *"Auguste Lumière died
on 10 April 1954"* and their terms claim none — the restriction is
contractual, and a contract binds the party who agreed to it. That is this
project, not you. So the realistic consequence of this reading being wrong
is that TMDB revokes our key, not that a claim reaches anyone downstream.

Two honest caveats. About 8% of published closings are dated by a death
only TMDB records; `poster/provenance.js` corroborated the rest against
Wikidata, which is CC0 already, and that share falls as more are matched.
And TMDB's terms require us to purge cached content if our licence ends —
an obligation we would honour, which is a reason to hold your own copy if
you are relying on this.

